        <div id="protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Which Has More Protein per Scoop, Clean Simple Eats or Levels?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Levels has more protein per scoop, 24 g against 20 g, because its scoop is bigger: 32 g of powder against 27 g. Gram for gram, the two are essentially level. About 75% of a Levels scoop is protein and about 74% of a Clean Simple Eats scoop is, per the labels we recorded.
          </p>

          <p>
            That surprises people, because Clean Simple Eats uses whey isolate, which is typically 85% to 90% protein before anything is added, while Levels uses concentrate, which is typically lower. The difference is what goes in alongside. Brownie Batter carries cocoa powder, coconut milk powder, sea salt and flavorings; Levels Vanilla Bean carries vanilla extract, lecithin, salt and sweeteners. The finished powders end up within a percentage point of each other.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, if you aim for 25 g of protein per shake, Levels gets you there in a scoop plus a little, about 33 g of powder and 135 calories. Clean Simple Eats needs about a scoop and a quarter, roughly 34 g of powder and 125 calories. If you just use one scoop and move on, you get 4 g more protein from Levels each time, which adds up to about 120 g over a month of daily shakes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Levels also publishes its amino acid profile, with 2.54 g of leucine per scoop, enough to sit in the 2 to 3 g range that research on muscle protein synthesis treats as a useful per-meal dose. Clean Simple Eats does not publish leucine. As a whey isolate its leucine share should be similar, but at 20 g of protein per scoop the total will be lower unless you use more powder. For daily targets, see <a href='/how-much-protein-do-i-need/'>how much protein you need</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

