        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Which Has the Cleaner Label?
          </h2>

          <p>
            They are close, and both are cleaner than most mainstream wheys. Neither uses sucralose, acesulfame potassium or any other artificial sweetener, and neither lists gums. Levels has six ingredients: whey protein concentrate, vanilla extract, sunflower lecithin, sea salt, stevia and monk fruit. Clean Simple Eats Brownie Batter has six plus an enzyme blend: grass-fed whey protein isolate, cocoa, natural flavors, sea salt, coconut milk powder and stevia.
          </p>

          <p>
            The differences are small and depend on what you avoid. Levels uses "vanilla extract" where Clean Simple Eats uses "natural flavors," a less specific term. Clean Simple Eats includes coconut milk powder, which matters to anyone avoiding coconut. Levels uses sunflower lecithin, which helps it mix and is soy-free, and adds monk fruit to the stevia, which some buyers find rounds off stevia's aftertaste. Clean Simple Eats advertises that it is free of xylitol and xanthan gum.
          </p>

          <p>
            On the nutrition panel, Clean Simple Eats is the leaner powder: 0.5 g of fat and under 1 g of sugar per scoop against 2.5 g and 2 g for Levels. That is the isolate at work. None of Levels' sugar is added; with no sugar on its ingredient list, those 2 g are lactose that comes with whey concentrate. For a low-carb or low-fat diet, Clean Simple Eats is the tidier fit, though the difference per shake is a few grams.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands describe their whey as grass-fed, and Levels also says hormone-free. These are brand claims rather than third-party certifications, and grass-fed whey has not been shown to build more muscle than conventional whey. If you prefer it for how the cows are raised, both qualify; if you want to widen the search, see our <a href='/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/'>protein powders without artificial sweeteners</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stomach" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Is Whey Isolate or Concentrate Better for You Here?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats is the safer pick for a sensitive stomach, because it is a whey isolate and adds a small digestive enzyme blend. Isolate is filtered to remove most of the lactose and fat, which is why its label shows under 1 g of sugar. Levels is a concentrate, which keeps more lactose: its 2 g of sugar per scoop is essentially all milk sugar.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people, 2 g of lactose is not a problem; it is a fraction of what a glass of milk carries, and many lactose-intolerant adults tolerate that much in a sitting. But if ordinary whey reliably gives you gas or bloating, the isolate is the better bet. The enzyme blend in Clean Simple Eats is small (100 mg in total, with the individual enzymes' amounts not disclosed), so treat it as a minor extra rather than the reason to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have never had trouble with whey, concentrate is not worse for you. It keeps a little more fat and some of the milk's other proteins, and it costs less to make, which is part of why Levels is cheaper. Our explainer on <a href='/whey-vs-whey-isolate/'>whey vs whey isolate</a> covers the trade-off in more depth, and our <a href='/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/'>lactose-free protein powder</a> guide lists options for people who need to avoid lactose entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both powders are made from cow's milk, so neither suits a milk allergy, which is different from lactose intolerance. If you have a diagnosed digestive condition, kidney disease, or are pregnant, ask a doctor or registered dietitian before adding a daily protein powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="taste" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Tastes Better, and Which Has More Flavors?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats is the flavor-first brand of the two, with about 12 whey flavors in our database against 9 for Levels. We have not tasted either powder; what follows is what buyers commonly report. Clean Simple Eats is built around dessert flavors such as Snickerdoodle, Cookies 'N Cream, Brownie Batter and Mint Chocolate Cookie, and flavor is the thing its buyers most often praise; the stevia sweetness is the thing a minority of buyers dislike.
          </p>

          <p>
            Levels' range is more conventional: Vanilla Bean, Double Chocolate, Pure Chocolate, Chocolate Peanut Butter, Strawberry, Vanilla Cinnamon, Cappuccino, Chocolate Mocha and Unflavored. Buyers commonly describe it as mild rather than intensely sweet, though we have not tallied reviews flavor by flavor. Its label includes sunflower lecithin, an emulsifier added to help powder disperse in a shaker, which Clean Simple Eats does not list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Both brands sell an unflavored version, which is the one to buy if you cook or bake with protein, since it adds no sweetness. Clean Simple Eats also sells Clear Protein, a juice-style whey isolate in flavors like Pink Burst and Blue Bahama, with 20 g of protein in a 28 g scoop and 100 calories per the brand's label. Levels has no clear protein in our database. If that style appeals, see our <a href='/best-clear-protein-powder/'>best clear protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Taste is personal, and stevia in particular divides people. If you know you dislike stevia, both of these will disappoint you, and our <a href='/best-protein-powder-without-stevia/'>protein powder without stevia</a> guide is the better starting point. If flavor is your deciding factor, our <a href='/best-tasting-protein-powder/'>best tasting protein powder</a> rankings weigh buyer feedback across brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cse-pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. What Clean Simple Eats Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clean Simple Eats does flavor and simplicity well at the same time, which few brands manage. It uses grass-fed whey isolate, sweetens only with stevia, skips gums and artificial sweeteners, and still offers a dozen dessert-style flavors. At 100 calories, 0.5 g of fat and under 1 g of sugar per scoop, it is one of the leaner flavored wheys in our database, and the isolate makes it the gentler choice for lactose.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where it falls short</h3>

          <p>
            Price and protein per scoop are the weak points. At about $2.71 per 25 g in the 30-serving bag, and more in the smaller bags, it is one of the more expensive wheys we track, and its 20 g scoop means you need more powder to reach a typical 25 to 30 g dose. Its Label Score of 48 reflects that: protein density is middling for an isolate and the cost component is close to the bottom of our scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            The testing claim is the other gap. "3rd party tested" with no named certifier and no published report is hard to check. Panels also differ between flavors, so the Brownie Batter numbers here will not match every bag; our <a href='/clean-simple-eats-protein-powder-review/'>Clean Simple Eats review</a> notes other flavors' labels. If you like the brand and want to compare it with another isolate, see <a href='/clean-simple-eats-vs-isopure/'>Clean Simple Eats vs Isopure</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="levels-pros-cons" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            12. What Levels Grass-Fed Whey Does Well and Where It Falls Short
          </h2>

          <p>
            Levels does the fundamentals at a fair price: 24 g of grass-fed whey protein per scoop, six ingredients, stevia and monk fruit instead of artificial sweeteners, soy-free, and about $1.91 per 25 g in the 5 lb tub. It publishes a full amino profile with 2.54 g of leucine. That combination is why its Label Score, 59, is the higher of the two here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where it falls short</h3>

          <p>
            As a concentrate it carries more lactose, fat and sugar than an isolate: 3 g of carbohydrate, 2 g of sugar and 2.5 g of fat per scoop. It has no certification we could verify, and the Clean Label Project award mentioned on its retail listing could not be confirmed for this product. Its Label Score is modest for the same reasons: average density and no certification points.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand itself is the bigger question mark. When our database was checked, the Levels website redirected to a domain-for-sale page, so label details, flavors and prices came from retailers. That does not change what is in the tub, but it means fewer ways to contact the brand or check a batch. For other Levels pairings, see <a href='/isopure-vs-levels/'>Isopure vs Levels</a> and <a href='/levels-vs-ascent-protein/'>Levels vs Ascent</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            13. Which Should You Buy, Clean Simple Eats or Levels?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy Levels if you want a grass-fed whey without artificial sweeteners and care about the price per gram. You get 24 g of protein in one scoop for about $1.91 per 25 g, a short ingredient list and a published leucine figure. It suits lifters who drink a shake most days and would rather put the $24 a month toward food.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy Clean Simple Eats if whey concentrate upsets your stomach, if you want the lowest fat and sugar per scoop, or if flavor is what keeps you drinking protein at all. The isolate and the dessert flavors are what you pay the premium for. It also suits people who want small bags rather than a 5 lb tub, though those cost more per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you cannot decide, ask how much protein you use. At one shake a week, the price gap is a few dollars a month and the flavor you will actually drink matters more. At two shakes a day, Levels saves you close to $50 a month. For the wider grass-fed field, see our <a href='/best-grass-fed-whey-protein/'>best grass-fed whey protein</a> rankings, which compare both against every grass-fed powder we track.
          </p>

          <p>
            If what you really want is grass-fed whey with nothing added at all, neither is the purest option. Naked Whey lists one ingredient; our sibling comparison <a href='/naked-vs-optimum-nutrition/'>Naked vs Optimum Nutrition</a> covers it against the best-known flavored whey. And if your choice is between Clean Simple Eats and another stevia-sweetened brand, see <a href='/flavcity-vs-clean-simple-eats/'>FlavCity vs Clean Simple Eats</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip-both" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            14. Who Should Skip Both?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are a drug-tested athlete. Neither brand had a sport certification we could verify, and a brand's own testing statement does not protect you if a batch fails. Choose a powder listed in the NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport databases instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip both if you dislike stevia. Both sweeten with it, Levels alongside monk fruit, and the aftertaste is the most common complaint about stevia-sweetened powders in general. The unflavored versions avoid the problem, as does any powder sweetened with sugar or sucralose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip both if you are vegan, dairy-free or allergic to milk; both are cow's-milk whey. Our <a href='/best-plant-based-protein-powder/'>best plant-based protein powder</a> guide is the place to start. And skip both if you want the cheapest protein per gram regardless of label: mainstream wheys with artificial sweeteners still undercut Levels in big tubs, as our <a href='/best-cheap-protein-powder/'>cheap protein powder</a> rankings show.
          </p>
        </div>

