          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            16. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing scoops instead of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A Clean Simple Eats scoop gives 20 g of protein and a Levels scoop gives 24 g, so "price per scoop" flatters Clean Simple Eats. Compare cost per 25 g instead: about $2.71 for Clean Simple Eats against about $1.91 for Levels at the prices we recorded.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming isolate means more protein per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clean Simple Eats uses isolate and Levels uses concentrate, yet both finished powders are about 74% to 75% protein by weight once cocoa, coconut milk powder, flavor and sweetener are added. The isolate buys you less lactose and fat, not a denser scoop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating "3rd party tested" as a certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clean Simple Eats says its products are third-party tested but names no certifier and publishes no report, and the award mentioned on Levels' retail listing could not be confirmed. Neither is a substitute for NSF Certified for Sport if your sport tests.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
